# A slot is born inside a specification

The owner's rule: **every application is written inside a specification — an OpenSpec change — and
any change without one is forbidden, except harness work.** The rule is the boundary on what a slot
*is*, so it is stated once here and cited everywhere else rather than restated.

It was violated on 2026-10-02: `apps/2026-10-02-laneset/` existed **with code** while
`openspec/changes/` held no change for it. The mechanism was mechanical, not deliberate. The three
recorded contracts a slot already needed — `economics.json`, `differentiators.json` and `naming.json`
— were each required by `scripts/new-slot.mjs`, validated before any file was written, and refused by
name when missing. **Nothing asked for a specification**, so a slot was born outside the spec the
owner required and every gate still returned `PASS`, because every gate read the contracts it had been
built from and none read the one that was absent.

## The rule

- **Every slot is created and built inside an OpenSpec change, authored by the planner before any app
  code exists.** The change lives at `openspec/changes/<change-name>/` and the slot's own change is
  `app-<slug>`. It carries a `proposal.md`, a `tasks.md` and at least one delta spec
  (`specs/<capability>/spec.md`); the shape is the existing `openspec/changes/app-lawn-round-log/`.
  The change is authored with the **`openspec-propose`** skill and is checked with
  `openspec validate <change-name>`; a change that does not validate is not a specification.
- **The scaffold refuses without it.** `scripts/new-slot.mjs` takes `--spec <change-name>`
  (defaulting to `app-<slug>`) and **refuses before it writes anything** when
  `openspec/changes/<spec>/` is missing, or lacks `proposal.md`, `tasks.md` or a `specs/**/spec.md`.
  The refusal fires before `mkdirSync`, because a slot directory that exists at all is half of the
  defect the rule prevents — the point is not to detect the violation afterwards but to make the
  ungoverned slot impossible to create. The refusal names the exact path it expected and the exact
  remedy.
- **The change stays live from scaffold to archive.** It is not a proposal written and forgotten: it
  is the specification the slot is built against, and it is the artifact a later reader checks the
  app against.
- **A killed or abandoned change is archived without merging its delta spec.** The rule and its
  instance list are stated in `AGENTS.md` ("A change whose capability was killed is archived without
  merging its delta spec") — that half is cited here, not restated, because the CLI only performs the
  merge and the distinction between a completed and a killed change is where the corruption lives.
- **Harness configuration is the one exception, and it is narrow.** Work on the factory itself —
  `scripts/**`, `.cursor/**`, `harness/**`, `docs/**`, `scaffold/**`, the registry — is outside the
  rule, because the factory is not one of its own slots and a change for every harness edit would
  make the harness unmaintainable. Nothing else is exempt: not "a small app", not "a prototype", not
  "the change would take as long as the code". A slot that is worth building is worth specifying.

## What is banned

- Creating a slot before its change exists, in any order — including scaffolding first and "writing
  the spec afterwards", because a specification written after the app only describes what was built.
- Adding application code to a slot whose change is absent, archived, or has never validated.
- Treating the change as optional paperwork, or importing the rule's *shape* (a proposal file) while
  leaving `tasks.md` or the delta spec out.
- Invoking the harness exception for anything under `apps/**`. The exception is for the factory, and
  `apps/**` is what the factory makes.

## How it is verified

- **`slot.spec`** is the enforcement: the check reads a slot for its originating change — the slot's
  declaration and the change under `openspec/changes/` — and fails when it is absent or malformed. It
  joins `scripts/new-slot.mjs`'s refusal, which is the *pre-creation* half: the scaffold cannot write
  a slot without a change, and the check reports one that got in some other way.
- **The scaffold refusal is the load-bearing half**, and it is stronger than any check that reads an
  existing slot, because it fires before the directory exists.
- Evidence and claims discipline are `.cursor/rules/verification-discipline.mdc` (a check that cannot
  fail is not a check) and `.cursor/rules/grounded-claims.mdc` (no claim without a source), cited
  rather than restated: this rule adds a boundary, not a second copy of how a boundary is proved.

### The honest limit

`slot.spec` proves a specification **exists and is well-formed**. It cannot prove the app matches it —
that the `tasks.md` were done, that the delta spec describes the shipped behaviour, or that the code
was written against the change rather than the change written around the code. A slot can hold a
valid, empty specification and pass. The judgement that the app is the app the specification
describes stays with the **owner at checkpoint 1**, alongside the money, naming and promise
decisions, exactly as the other contracts' honest limits state. What the gate removes is the
*unnoticed* ungoverned slot — the one that never had a specification at all, and was therefore never
asked what it was for.
